bwfm_pci -- FreeBSD driver for Broadcom FullMAC PCIe wireless chips
===================================================================

Hardware
--------

bwfm_pci probes these Broadcom PCI device IDs (vendor 14e4):

    43ba  BCM43602   full bring-up; the supported chip
    43a0  BCM4360    probe only
    43a2  BCM4360    2x2, probe only
    43a3  BCM4350    probe only
    4415, 43c3, 43ca, 4464  (BCM4366/4365/4364 family) probe only

Only the BCM43602 is brought up.  It has been tested on the Apple
MacBook Pro 11,4 (subsystem 106b:0152), revision A1.  The BCM4360 has
no ARM-CR4 FullMAC firmware available, so bring-up is refused on it;
see sys/modules/bwfm_pci_fw_4360/Makefile.

The BCM43602 firmware, brcmfmac43602-pcie.bin, is shipped as the
bwfm_pci_fw_43602 module and registered under the firmware(9) name
brcmfmac43602_pcie.

Loading
-------

    kldload bwfm_pci_fw_43602 bwfm_pci

At boot, load it from rc.conf rather than loader.conf:

    kld_list="bwfm_pci_fw_43602 bwfm_pci"

kld_list loads the modules once the system is up.  Preloaded from
loader.conf, the driver attaches during boot-time autoconfiguration,
and a chip left wedged by an earlier hang can then stop the boot with
nothing logged.  Loaded later, the same failure leaves a running system
to recover from.

Attach does no BAR0 access until the warm-up sequence has run: on the
Apple machines the chip sits behind an EC-controlled power gate, and a
BAR0 read before it is opened master-aborts the host.

Bring-up
--------

At the end of attach a kernel thread uploads the firmware (unless it is
already running), attaches the msgbuf rings, sends WLC_UP and attaches
to net80211.  This takes about nine seconds; kldload returns at once.  The
device then appears in net.wlan.devices, and only wlan0 is left to
create:

    ifconfig wlan0 create wlandev bwfm_pci0

wlans_bwfm_pci0 in rc.conf is too early, since netif runs before the
bring-up has finished.

Taking the last vap down leaves the network and turns the radio off
with WLC_DOWN; bringing one up turns it back on with WLC_UP.  The
firmware stays loaded in between.

Loader tunables
---------------

    hw.bwfm_pci.autostart=0
        Leave the chip down after attach.

    hw.bwfm_pci.attach_appu_warm=0
        Skip the warm-up sequence at attach (default 1).

    hw.bwfm_pci.pm_supported=1
        Allow suspend/resume attempts (default 0, suspend is refused).
        Warm resume is best effort; a cold resume still needs
        kldunload and kldload.

dev.bwfm_pci_nvram_seed_enable (default 0) adds a random-seed prelude to
the NVRAM image.  It is off because it stopped the BCM43602 firmware
from coming up.

Sysctls (dev.bwfm_pci.0)
------------------------

    debug                  log verbosity (0 = quiet)
    net80211_detach        detach from net80211 before kldunload
    crash_recover_threshold
                           consecutive command timeouts before an
                           automatic firmware re-upload and re-bring-up
                           (default 0, off: a timeout does not prove the
                           firmware crashed, and rebuilding a running
                           chip can hang the host)

Security
--------

WPA2-PSK with host EAPOL: wpa_supplicant runs the 4-way handshake and
installs the keys through net80211.  Tested with CCMP pairwise and TKIP
group ciphers.  The BCM43602 firmware does not support SAE (WPA3) or
FT.

Known limits
------------

- Detach is refused while net80211 is attached: destroy wlan0 and
  write 1 to net80211_detach before kldunload.
- After the wireless power rail has been fully power-cycled, the
  firmware has been seen to halt during PA calibration.
- Only one access point has been tested.
